        "content": "# Design Decisions: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator\n**Created**: 2026-01-31\n\n## Decision Log\n\n| ID | Decision | Choice | Rationale | Alternatives Considered |\n|----|----------|--------|-----------|------------------------|\n| D1 | Parser architecture | Two-phase (lexer + parser) | Clear separation of concerns: lexer handles character-level processing and position tracking, parser handles syntax validation. Easier to maintain and test independently. Error positions are naturally tracked by lexer. | Single-phase regex-based parser (harder to track positions), recursive descent parser (more complex for syntax-only validation) |\n| D2 | YAML subset scope | Block style only (no flow collections) | Covers 90%+ of configuration file use cases. Flow style (`[a,b]`, `{a:b}`) adds significant complexity for marginal benefit. Can be added later if needed. | Full YAML 1.2 support (too complex without external library), minimal subset (too limiting) |\n| D3 | AST construction | No AST, stream validation | Requirements specify syntax validation only, not semantic analysis. Stream validation is simpler and sufficient. Avoids memory overhead of tree construction. | Full AST construction (overkill for syntax validation, but would enable future features like reformatting) |\n| D4 | Module organization | `src/yaml/` for parser, `src/cli/` for CLI | Follows existing project pattern of flat `src/` structure. Separates reusable YAML parser from CLI-specific code. Parser could be imported by other modules in future. | Single file (poor separation), `src/lib/yaml/` (deeper nesting than existing patterns) |\n| D5 | Error reporting count | First error only (fail-fast) | Explicitly allowed by requirements (BR-004). Simpler implementation without error recovery. Most syntax errors cascade, so subsequent errors are often noise. | Collect all errors (requires error recovery, more complex), first N errors (arbitrary cutoff) |\n| D6 | Indentation handling | Spaces only, tabs rejected | YAML 1.2 spec recommends spaces. Mixing tabs and spaces causes subtle bugs. Rejecting tabs early prevents confusion. | Support tabs (convert to spaces), support both (complexity), configurable (over-engineering) |\n| D7 | Position indexing | 1-indexed line and column | Industry standard for error messages. Matches editor line numbers. Consistent with requirements (REQ-004). | 0-indexed (programmer convention but confusing in error messages) |\n| D8 | File reading | Bun.file() async API | Native Bun API, efficient streaming support, built-in existence check. Matches project's Bun-first approach. | Node.js fs module (adds dependency on Node compatibility), sync reading (blocks event loop) |\n| D9 | Test organization | Co-located with source (`*.test.ts`) | Follows existing project pattern (`utils.test.ts`). Easy to find related tests. Supported by Bun test runner. | Separate `tests/` directory (breaks existing pattern), `__tests__/` directory (Jest convention, not used here) |\n| D10 | Block scalar parsing | Lexer emits indicator, parser handles content | Block scalars (`|`, `>`) have complex indentation rules. Lexer identifies the indicator; parser validates content indentation based on context. Cleaner separation than trying to handle in lexer alone. | Lexer handles full block scalar (requires lookahead, complex state), defer to runtime (not validating syntax) |\n\n## Design Rationale Summary\n\n### Why Two-Phase Architecture?\n\nThe lexer + parser design was chosen because:\n\n1. **Position Tracking**: The lexer naturally tracks line/column as it processes characters. This information flows to tokens and ultimately to error messages without additional bookkeeping.\n\n2. **Testability**: Each phase can be tested independently. Lexer tests verify tokenization. Parser tests verify syntax rules using token arrays.\n\n3. **Error Isolation**: Lexical errors (unclosed quotes, invalid characters) are caught early. Syntax errors (bad indentation, invalid structure) are caught by parser with meaningful context.\n\n4. **YAML Complexity**: YAML's significant whitespace and context-sensitivity benefit from lexer preprocessing. The parser receives a clean token stream rather than raw characters.\n\n### Why Not Full YAML 1.2?\n\nFull YAML 1.2 compliance requires:\n- Flow collections with nested structures\n- Anchor/alias resolution\n- Tag handling and type coercion\n- Complex key support\n- Multi-document streams\n\nThese features add significant implementation complexity with diminishing returns for a syntax validator. The chosen subset covers typical configuration files while keeping the implementation manageable without external libraries.\n\n### Why No AST?\n\nFor syntax validation, we only need to answer: \"Is this valid YAML?\" Building an AST would answer: \"What does this YAML represent?\" The latter is unnecessary overhead when the goal is detecting malformed input. Stream validation consumes tokens and validates rules without storing the document structure.\n"
