        "file_path": "/private/tmp/rp1-evals/6f34a28f/workspace/.rp1/work/features/yaml-validator/requirements.md",
        "content": "# Requirements Specification: YAML Validator Command\n\n**Feature ID**: yaml-validator\n**Version**: 1.0.0\n**Status**: Draft\n**Created**: 2026-01-31\n\n## 1. Feature Overview\n\nA command-line tool that validates YAML files for syntax correctness without relying on external dependencies. The validator checks whether a YAML file is well-formed and reports syntax errors with precise line and column information, enabling developers to quickly identify and fix malformed YAML in their projects.\n\n## 2. Business Context\n\n### 2.1 Problem Statement\n\nDevelopers working with YAML configuration files need a quick way to validate syntax without installing additional dependencies or using external tools. Currently, syntax errors in YAML files are often only discovered at runtime when applications fail to parse them, leading to delayed feedback and debugging overhead.\n\n### 2.2 Business Value\n\n- Reduces time spent debugging YAML syntax errors by providing immediate validation feedback\n- Maintains minimal project footprint by avoiding external parsing library dependencies\n- Integrates seamlessly into development workflows and CI/CD pipelines via standard exit codes\n- Demonstrates custom parser implementation capabilities within the project\n\n### 2.3 Success Metrics\n\n| Metric | Target |\n|--------|--------|\n| Syntax error detection rate | 100% of malformed YAML detected |\n| Error location accuracy | Line and column numbers reported for all errors |\n| Exit code compliance | Consistent exit 0 (valid) / exit 1 (invalid) behavior |\n| Dependency count | Zero new runtime or dev dependencies added |\n\n## 3. Stakeholders & Users\n\n### 3.1 User Types\n\n| User Type | Description | Primary Needs |\n|-----------|-------------|---------------|\n| Developer | Software developers working with YAML configuration files | Quick syntax validation, clear error messages with location info |\n\n### 3.2 Stakeholder Interests\n\n| Stakeholder | Interest |\n|-------------|----------|\n| Development Team | Maintain minimal dependency footprint while adding useful tooling |\n| Project Maintainers | Ensure new features align with project's minimalist philosophy |\n\n## 4. Scope Definition\n\n### 4.1 In Scope\n\n- CLI command for validating YAML file syntax\n- Custom YAML parser implementation (no external dependencies)\n- Plain text error output with line and column numbers\n- Exit code 0 for valid YAML files\n- Exit code 1 for invalid YAML files\n- Single file validation per invocation\n\n### 4.2 Out of Scope\n\n- Schema validation (checking YAML against a schema definition)\n- Multi-file batch validation\n- Standard input (stdin) support\n- YAML formatting or pretty-printing\n- YAML to JSON conversion or vice versa\n- Validation of YAML semantic correctness beyond syntax\n- Configuration file support for the validator itself\n- Watch mode or file monitoring\n- Integration with IDEs or editors\n\n### 4.3 Assumptions\n\n- Users have Bun runtime installed and available\n- Input files exist and are readable by the process\n- Files are encoded in UTF-8\n- YAML 1.2 syntax subset is sufficient for validation needs\n\n## 5. Functional Requirements\n\n### REQ-001: File Path Input\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer\n**Requirement**: The validator accepts a file path as a command-line argument specifying the YAML file to validate.\n**Rationale**: Developers need to specify which file to validate when running the command.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Command accepts a single positional argument for the file path\n- File path can be absolute or relative to current working directory\n- Missing file path argument results in appropriate error message and exit code 1\n\n### REQ-002: File Existence Validation\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer\n**Requirement**: The validator checks that the specified file exists before attempting to parse it.\n**Rationale**: Clear error messaging for non-existent files improves developer experience.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Non-existent file results in error message indicating file not found\n- Error message includes the file path that was not found\n- Exit code is 1 for non-existent files\n\n### REQ-003: YAML Syntax Parsing\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer\n**Requirement**: The validator parses the YAML file content to determine if it is syntactically well-formed.\n**Rationale**: Core functionality to detect malformed YAML before it causes runtime errors.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Valid YAML syntax is recognized and accepted\n- Common YAML constructs are supported: mappings, sequences, scalars, comments, multi-line strings\n- Parser is implemented without external YAML parsing libraries\n\n### REQ-004: Error Location Reporting\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer\n**Requirement**: When syntax errors are detected, the validator reports the line number and column number where the error occurred.\n**Rationale**: Precise error locations enable developers to quickly locate and fix issues.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Error output includes line number (1-indexed)\n- Error output includes column number (1-indexed)\n- Line and column accurately point to the location of the syntax error\n\n### REQ-005: Plain Text Error Output\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer\n**Requirement**: Error messages are output as plain text to standard error (stderr).\n**Rationale**: Plain text is universally readable and easily parsed by other tools if needed.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Error messages are human-readable plain text\n- Errors are written to stderr\n- Format includes file path, line number, column number, and error description\n\n### REQ-006: Success Output\n**Priority**: Should Have\n**User Type**: Developer\n**Requirement**: When validation succeeds, the validator outputs a confirmation message.\n**Rationale**: Explicit success confirmation provides clear feedback to the user.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Success message indicates the file is valid YAML\n- Success message is written to stdout\n- Message includes the validated file path\n\n### REQ-007: Exit Code - Valid File\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer\n**Requirement**: The validator exits with code 0 when the YAML file is syntactically valid.\n**Rationale**: Standard exit code convention enables integration with scripts and CI/CD pipelines.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Exit code is exactly 0 for valid YAML files\n- Exit code 0 is returned regardless of YAML content complexity\n\n### REQ-008: Exit Code - Invalid File\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer\n**Requirement**: The validator exits with code 1 when the YAML file contains syntax errors or cannot be validated.\n**Rationale**: Standard exit code convention enables integration with scripts and CI/CD pipelines.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- Exit code is exactly 1 for invalid YAML files\n- Exit code is 1 for file access errors\n- Exit code is 1 for missing arguments\n\n### REQ-009: No New Dependencies\n**Priority**: Must Have\n**User Type**: Developer\n**Requirement**: The validator is implemented without adding any new dependencies to the project.\n**Rationale**: Maintains the project's minimal dependency philosophy and demonstrates custom implementation capabilities.\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- No new entries added to package.json dependencies\n- No new entries added to package.json devDependencies\n- YAML parsing logic is implemented from scratch\n\n## 6. Non-Functional Requirements\n\n### 6.1 Performance Expectations\n\n| Aspect | Expectation |\n|--------|-------------|\n| Validation Speed | Typical config files (under 1000 lines) validate in under 100ms |\n| Memory Usage | Memory usage proportional to file size, no excessive buffering |\n\n### 6.2 Security Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| File Access | Only read access to specified file; no write operations |\n| Path Traversal | Handle file paths safely without exposing system vulnerabilities |\n\n### 6.3 Usability Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| Error Clarity | Error messages clearly describe the syntax issue encountered |\n| Location Precision | Line/column numbers accurately point to error location |\n| Minimal Learning Curve | Command usage follows standard CLI conventions |\n\n### 6.4 Compliance Requirements\n\n| Requirement | Description |\n|-------------|-------------|\n| YAML Specification | Parser handles common YAML 1.2 syntax constructs |\n| Exit Code Standards | Follows Unix convention (0 = success, non-zero = failure) |\n\n## 7. User Stories\n\n### STORY-001: Validate Configuration File\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to validate a YAML configuration file from the command line\n**So that** I can ensure the file has correct syntax before using it\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN a valid YAML file exists at the specified path\n- WHEN the developer runs the validator with the file path\n- THEN the validator outputs a success message and exits with code 0\n\n### STORY-002: Identify Syntax Error Location\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to see the exact line and column of a YAML syntax error\n**So that** I can quickly navigate to and fix the problem\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN a YAML file with a syntax error on line 15, column 8\n- WHEN the developer runs the validator with the file path\n- THEN the error output includes \"line 15\" and \"column 8\" (or equivalent)\n- AND the validator exits with code 1\n\n### STORY-003: Handle Missing File\n**As a** developer\n**I want** to receive a clear error when the specified file does not exist\n**So that** I can correct the file path\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN no file exists at the specified path\n- WHEN the developer runs the validator with that path\n- THEN an error message indicates the file was not found\n- AND the validator exits with code 1\n\n### STORY-004: CI Pipeline Integration\n**As a** developer\n**I want** the validator to use standard exit codes\n**So that** I can integrate it into CI/CD pipelines that check exit codes\n\n**Acceptance Criteria**:\n- GIVEN a CI pipeline that runs the validator\n- WHEN the YAML file is valid\n- THEN the pipeline step succeeds (exit code 0)\n- AND WHEN the YAML file is invalid\n- THEN the pipeline step fails (exit code 1)\n\n## 8. Business Rules\n\n| Rule ID | Rule Description |\n|---------|------------------|\n| BR-001 | A file is considered valid only if it contains no YAML syntax errors |\n| BR-002 | Empty files are considered valid YAML (empty document) |\n| BR-003 | Files containing only comments are considered valid YAML |\n| BR-004 | Only the first syntax error needs to be reported (fail-fast behavior acceptable) |\n| BR-005 | The validator must not modify the input file under any circumstances |\n\n## 9. Dependencies & Constraints\n\n### Dependencies\n\n| Dependency | Type | Description |\n|------------|------|-------------|\n| Bun Runtime | Runtime | Required to execute the TypeScript/JavaScript code |\n| File System Access | System | Required to read the input YAML file |\n\n### Constraints\n\n| Constraint | Description |\n|------------|-------------|\n| No External YAML Libraries | Must implement YAML parsing without libraries like js-yaml, yaml, etc. |\n| Single File Operation | Each invocation validates exactly one file |\n| Synchronous Operation | File reading and validation complete before process exits |\n| TypeScript Implementation | Code must be written in TypeScript consistent with project standards |\n\n## 10. Clarifications Log\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|----------|--------|--------|\n| What format for error output? | Plain text errors with line/column numbers | User clarification |\n| Multi-file support needed? | No, single file only (one file at a time) | User clarification |\n| What level of validation? | Syntax only (check YAML is well-formed, no schema validation) | User clarification |\n| Support stdin input? | No, files only | User clarification |\n| What exit codes to use? | Exit 0 for valid, exit 1 for invalid | User clarification |\n| Who are the primary users? | Developers | User clarification |\n\n## AFK Mode: Auto-Selected Defaults\n\n| Decision Point | Choice | Rationale |\n|----------------|--------|-----------|\n| PRD selection | None | No PRDs found in .rp1/work/prds/ directory |\n| KB context | Best-effort from project files | KB files (index.md, concept_map.md) do not exist |\n\n## AFK Mode: Inferred Decisions\n\n| Ambiguity | Resolution | Source |\n|-----------|------------|--------|\n| YAML version to support | YAML 1.2 subset | Industry standard, conservative default |\n| Error reporting count | First error only (fail-fast) | Conservative default for simple validator |\n| Empty file handling | Treat as valid | YAML spec allows empty documents |\n| Success message destination | stdout | Unix convention (errors to stderr, output to stdout) |\n| File encoding | UTF-8 | Project standard assumption |\n"
      },
      "source": "assistant"
    },
